        <item>
            <title>MacTR 1.3.16</title>
            <sparkle:version>1.3.16</sparkle:version>
            <sparkle:shortVersionString>1.3.16</sparkle:shortVersionString>
            <description><![CDATA[
                <style>
                    body { font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, sans-serif; font-size: 13px; color: #333; padding: 8px; }
                    h3 { font-size: 14px; margin: 8px 0 4px; }
                    ul { padding-left: 20px; margin: 4px 0; }
                    li { margin: 2px 0; }
                </style>
                <h3>v1.3.16 — core types read from the hardware</h3>
                <ul>
                    <li><b>Core layout is now detected at runtime</b> from the IODeviceTree, where every CPU reports its own <code>cluster-type</code> (E or P) alongside the logical id that indexing uses. Nothing in the code assumes an ordering any more.</li>
                    <li><b>Correct on any Apple Silicon</b> — M1/M2/M3/M4, any core split, and any future part that orders its clusters differently, with no code change.</li>
                    <li>Labels shown on screen and named in alerts now come from the same hardware-reported source, so they cannot disagree.</li>
                </ul>
                <p>Background: v1.1.0 through v1.3.11 derived core positions from <code>hw.perflevel0.logicalcpu</code>, which is a <i>count</i>, and assumed performance cores came first because perflevel <i>0</i> is named "Performance". That number ranks performance tiers; it is not an array offset. Asking the device tree removes the guess entirely.</p>
            ]]></description>
            <pubDate>Sun, 02 Aug 2026 23:40:00 +0900</pubDate>
            <enclosure
                url="https://github.com/beret21/MacTR/releases/download/v1.3.16/MacTR-1.3.16.zip"
                sparkle:edSignature="ch5RhryAlxEAhrbhD6EILqwhXiy7rSV/sfFjwYoSKqJjojiTKrrbjU2wjb0d1XyOrB0ZK4+20euOZb0OJp9ZBg=="
                length="1811821"
                type="application/octet-stream" />
        </item>

        <item>
            <title>MacTR 1.3.15</title>
            <sparkle:version>1.3.15</sparkle:version>
            <sparkle:shortVersionString>1.3.15</sparkle:shortVersionString>
            <description><![CDATA[
                <style>
                    body { font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, sans-serif; font-size: 13px; color: #333; padding: 8px; }
                    h3 { font-size: 14px; margin: 8px 0 4px; }
                    ul { padding-left: 20px; margin: 4px 0; }
                    li { margin: 2px 0; }
                </style>
                <h3>v1.3.15 — adaptive process scanning</h3>
                <ul>
                    <li><b>MacTR no longer polls harder than it needs to.</b> Walking every process is the only expensive thing it does on a timer (~7ms for ~850 processes); it used to run every 2 seconds regardless of whether anything was happening.</li>
                    <li><b>The pace now follows the load.</b> Idle: every 60s. A core saturates: 2s, immediately, so the responsible process can be named. Once it clears the interval relaxes 2 → 5 → 15 → 60s. Roughly 5× fewer scans while idle.</li>
                    <li><b>New setting (General → 감시 강도)</b>: 절전 (120s fixed) / 표준 (adaptive, default) / 정밀 (2s fixed).</li>
                    <li>Opening the menu bar menu briefly tightens the scan — you are looking, so the numbers should be current.</li>
                </ul>
                <p>Core, memory and temperature readings are unaffected and still refresh every 0.5s: each is a single syscall and costs nothing worth pacing.</p>
            ]]></description>
            <pubDate>Sun, 02 Aug 2026 23:00:00 +0900</pubDate>
            <enclosure
                url="https://github.com/beret21/MacTR/releases/download/v1.3.15/MacTR-1.3.15.zip"
                sparkle:edSignature="4dqgTWwXQM9UpXlN1OZ8zd40FFyOsPhc2og6d5XVaQV7+XuPsnSCuG0pd1pMcBjSCcT8Cz6UyAshnwFQ18x/Bw=="
